SETS.append(("Medium", "z1-rc5", "Passage: What a city tree is worth", P(
 "Ask a city council why it plants trees and you will usually hear a list of services: shade that cools the street, leaves that trap dust, roots that soak up storm water, and branches that give birds somewhere to live. Each of these benefits can be measured, and some cities now publish reports that put a money value on their trees, adding up the electricity saved on air-conditioning and the drainage costs avoided. The totals are impressive, often running into large sums for a single city, and they have helped tree budgets survive in years when other spending was cut.",
 "There is a risk in this way of arguing, however. Once a tree is defended as a service provider, it can be judged like any other provider, and replaced if a cheaper one appears. A drainage engineer may point out that an underground tank handles storm water more reliably than roots. A shade sail is cheaper to install than a tree is to raise. On a narrow spreadsheet, the tree begins to lose.",
 "The spreadsheet also struggles with time. A newly planted sapling provides almost none of the listed services; a mature tree provides them in abundance. The value of the young tree lies almost entirely in a future that the yearly budget does not see. When money is short, it is tempting to cut the maintenance that keeps saplings alive, since the loss will not show up in the accounts for decades. Many cities have planted generously and then watched a large share of their new trees die within a few years.",
 "None of this is an argument against measurement. Numbers have rescued many trees, and a council that knows how much its canopy is worth makes better decisions than one that does not. The point is that the numbers capture only the part of a tree's value that resembles a utility. People also value trees for reasons that resist pricing: the sense of seasons changing on an ordinary street, the memory of a particular tree outside a childhood home, the feeling that a neighbourhood is cared for. These are not soft extras, to be mentioned once the serious accounting is done. They are often what residents mention first when a tree is threatened.",
 "A sensible policy would use the numbers as a floor rather than a ceiling, a minimum case that any tree can make for itself, while leaving room for the reasons that cannot be counted."),
 [("The author's main concern about valuing trees by their services is that",
   ["the services of trees are too difficult to measure.", "it invites replacing trees with cheaper alternatives that provide the same service.", "it makes tree budgets too large.", "it ignores the cost of planting trees."], 1,
   "Paragraph 2: once trees are defended as service providers they 'can be judged like any other provider, and replaced if a cheaper one appears' (tanks, shade sails). Option 1 contradicts 'Each of these benefits can be measured'."),
  ("Why, according to the passage, do many newly planted city trees die?",
   ["They are planted in unsuitable soil.", "Maintenance for saplings is cut because their value lies in a future the yearly budget does not see.", "Residents remove them because they block light.", "Storm water damages their roots."], 1,
   "Paragraph 3: the young tree's value is in the future, so when money is short it is tempting to cut sapling maintenance, and many new trees die. The other causes are not mentioned."),
  ("Which of the following, if true, would most weaken the author's argument in paragraph 2?",
   ["Underground tanks are expensive to maintain.", "In practice, cities that publish money values for trees have planted more trees, not fewer, and have not replaced trees with engineered alternatives.", "Shade sails are popular in hot countries.", "Birds prefer old trees to young ones."], 1,
   "Paragraph 2 warns that service-based valuation leads to trees being replaced by cheaper substitutes. Evidence that cities using such valuations have not done so, and have planted more trees, directly undercuts that risk. Option 1 mildly helps trees but does not address the claimed tendency."),
  ("What does the author mean by using the numbers 'as a floor rather than a ceiling'?",
   ["Trees should be valued only by their measurable services.", "Measured services should set the minimum case for a tree, with unmeasurable values added on top.", "Councils should spend as little as possible on trees.", "Tree budgets should never be cut below a fixed level."], 1,
   "A floor is a minimum, a ceiling a maximum. The sentence adds 'a minimum case … while leaving room for the reasons that cannot be counted'. Option 1 treats the numbers as a ceiling, the opposite."),
 ],
 [("pj", ["That stability, rather than any single brilliant player, explained most of their success.", "Analysts studying a decade of results found that the most successful clubs were not those that spent the most on transfers.", "They were the ones that changed managers least often and kept a settled core of players.", "Spending still mattered, but only when it was combined with continuity."], "2314",
   "2 introduces the finding in negative form. 3 ('They were the ones…') completes it. 1 ('That stability…') interprets it. 4 qualifies the role of spending. 1 must follow 3 because 'That stability' refers to 3's settled managers and players."),
  ("pj", ["The second group, given no such instruction, remembered far less of it.", "In one experiment, two groups of volunteers read the same short story.", "One group was told beforehand that they would have to explain the story to a child.", "Preparing to teach, it seems, changes the way we take information in."], "2314",
   "2 sets up the experiment. 3 describes the first group's instruction. 1 ('The second group, given no such instruction') contrasts with 3. 4 draws the conclusion."),
  ("os", ["Early weather forecasts relied heavily on the experience of individual forecasters.", "Modern forecasts are built from computer models that simulate the atmosphere.", "Forecasters still add judgement, especially for local effects that models handle poorly.", "Barometers measure air pressure using a column of liquid or a sealed metal box.", "The best forecasts today come from combining models with that human judgement."], "4",
   "Sentences 1, 2, 3 and 5 trace forecasting from personal experience to models plus judgement. Sentence 4 describes how barometers work, a technical detail outside that thread."),
  ("sum", "Warnings on products are meant to protect consumers, but when every product carries a long list of them, people stop reading. A warning about a genuine danger is then lost among warnings about trivial ones. Fewer, sharper warnings would therefore protect people better than the current flood.",
   ["Products should carry no warnings at all.", "Too many warnings dilute attention, so fewer and clearer warnings would protect consumers better.", "Consumers are careless about safety.", "Manufacturers add warnings mainly to avoid lawsuits."], 1,
   "Option 2 has the problem (dilution) and the proposed fix. Option 1 is extreme. Option 3 blames consumers, which the paragraph does not do. Option 4 introduces a motive not discussed."),
  ("sum", "A language's grammar is often taught as a set of rules handed down from authority. In fact most grammatical rules began as descriptions of how people already spoke. Over time, some descriptions hardened into commands, and the usage that had produced them moved on, leaving rules that describe how no one now speaks.",
   ["Grammar rules are useless.", "Many grammar rules started as descriptions of usage, hardened into commands, and fell out of step as usage changed.", "People should speak as they please.", "Grammar is invented by authorities."], 1,
   "Option 2 traces the whole process the paragraph describes. Options 1 and 3 overstate. Option 4 contradicts the paragraph."),
  ("comp", "A town introduced a fine for parents who arrived late to collect their children from a day-care centre. To the town's surprise, late arrivals increased. ____ Removing the fine later did not restore the old behaviour, because the sense of obligation had already been replaced by a price.",
   ["The fine was too small to matter to most parents.", "Parents seemed to treat the fine as a fee for extra time, which relieved them of the guilt they had felt before.", "Many parents worked longer hours that year.", "The day-care staff did not enforce the fine."], 1,
   "The last sentence says the 'sense of obligation had already been replaced by a price'. Option 2 supplies exactly that mechanism. Option 1 could explain no change but not the replacement of obligation by price."),
 ]))

def build(B):
    import re
    for lv, sid, title, html, rcs, vas in SETS:
        words = len(re.sub(r"<[^>]+>", " ", html).split()); assert 400 <= words <= 620, (sid, words)
        B.add_set(sid, "passage", title, f"<b>{title}</b>" + html)
        for q, opts, ans, sol in rcs: B.mcq("varc", "Reading Comprehension", lv, q, opts, opts[ans], sol, set_id=sid)
        for it in vas:
            if it[0] == "pj":
                _, sents, ans, sol = it; assert len(sents) == 4
                B.tita("varc", "Para-jumbles", lv, PJ_HEAD + ol(sents), ans, f"Correct order {ans}. " + sol, kind="sequence")
            elif it[0] == "os":
                _, sents, ans, sol = it; assert len(sents) == 5
                B.tita("varc", "Odd sentence out", lv, OS_HEAD + ol(sents), ans, sol, kind="digit")
            elif it[0] == "sum":
                _, para, opts, ans, sol = it
                B.mcq("varc", "Para-summary", lv, SUM_HEAD + f"<p>{para}</p>", opts, opts[ans], sol)
            elif it[0] == "comp":
                _, para, opts, ans, sol = it; assert "____" in para
                B.mcq("varc", "Para-completion", lv, COMP_HEAD + f"<p>{para}</p>", opts, opts[ans], sol)
